 ```
 
+### Live services pane on SSH login (`full` profile)
+
+The `full` profile automatically attaches interactive Bash/Zsh SSH logins to a
+persistent tmux session named `lab`. The shell stays focused in the upper pane;
+the lower pane shows a live inventory, refreshed every **5 seconds**:
+
+- All Docker containers: name, image, published/internal ports and status,
+  including health checks and stopped containers.
+- Non-Docker listening TCP/UDP services, identified from their process names
+  (including Python modules), plus the running/failed systemd service counts.
+
+Use **Ctrl-b, then Down** to focus the services pane, **Up/Down** or **PgUp/PgDn**
+to scroll, **r** to refresh, and **Ctrl-b, then z** to zoom/unzoom. **Ctrl-b, then
+Up** returns to the shell. Reconnecting reuses the session without duplicating
+the pane; a closed services pane is restored on the next connection. Normal
+tmux defaults apply (including **Ctrl-b, then d** to detach).
+
+The profile settings are `ssh_dashboard_enabled`, `ssh_dashboard_session_name`,
+`ssh_dashboard_refresh_seconds` and `ssh_dashboard_pane_percent` (default: 45%).
+Set `LAB_SSH_DASHBOARD=0` before the managed block in the guest's `.bashrc` or
+`.zshrc` to opt out of automatic attachment. The table is also available as
+`lab-services` (one-off) or `lab-services --watch 5` (interactive).
+
+To add/update just this feature on an already-provisioned VM, from `kali-lab/`:
+
+```bash
+venv/bin/ansible-playbook -i '<vm-ip>,' -u kali provision/site.yml \
+  -e @provision/profiles/full.yml --tags ssh_dashboard
+```
+
+The dashboard uses Python's standard library, `ss` and `ps`; it tolerates Docker
+being absent/unavailable. Where existing sudo permissions allow it, passwordless
+read-only queries identify sockets owned by other users. Remote commands and
+non-interactive SSH sessions keep their usual behaviour.
+
